title: One-Shot Timers
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex09_OneShot2P.em.ts
  - em.core:em.hal/OneShotI.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex08_OneShot1P.em.ts

steps:

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - open 1
    focus: [1,1]
    text: |

        {[ht,$start]%[cu]OneShotP% &ndash; Timer Events}

        Once again, we'll blink `AppLed` &ndash; but now we'll use a {[cu]OneShot} {[cs]$delegate} to sleep for a given period of time.

        This tour actually covers two example programs:

        {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]OneShot1P}, which uses a primitive event loop<br>
        {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]OneShot2P}, which relies upon fiber dispatch

        Because of its similarity to the {[cu]FiberP} program viewed in the prior tour, we'll begin with {[cu]OneShot2P}.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 A 9
      - mark 1 B 34
      - mark 1 C 39
    focus: [1,8]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

        {[ht,$details]%[cu]OneShot2P% &ndash; Details}

        Like our earlier {[cu]FiberP} example, {[cu]OneShot2P} defines a {[cf]blinkFB} callback dispatched by {[cu]FiberMgr}.

        <div class="em-bmlist">

        {[bm,A]} The {[cu]OneShot} {[cs]$delegate} exported by {[cu]BoardC} imple&shy;ments the {[cu]OneShotI} **Interface** (coming next).

        {[bm,B]} {[cf]uenable} arms an underlying HW timer to sleep for *n* &mu;s and trigger a {[cn]handler} callback upon awakening.

        {[bm,C]} The {[cf]handler} function itself then calls {[cf]post}, which in turn schedules the next cycle of {[cf]blinkFB}.

        </div>

        In this example, {[cu]FiberMgr} detects an *empty* internal {[cn]ready_list} after our {[cf]blinkFB} fiber body returns.

        Recalling its implementation, {[cu]FiberMgr} then enters a HW sleep mode via the {[cu]Common}.{[cn]Idle} proxy.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 2
      - mark 2 B 4
      - mark 2 C 6
    focus: [2,1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

        {[ht,$details]%[cu]OneShotI% &ndash; Details}

        While you may have browsed to other `em.hal` inter&shy;faces, let's use {[cu]OneShotI} to explain this **EMS** pattern.

        <div class="em-bmlist">

        {[bm,A]} Similar to boilerplate found in every **EMS Unit**, we here {[cs]$declare} the "flavor" of {[cu]OneShotI}.

        {[bm,B]} In general, an **EMS Interface** can define *constants* and *types* which support its published methods.

        {[bm,C]} A **_TypeScript_** interface conventionally named {[cs]$I} specifies the signature of every client function.

        </div>

        In contrast to a **Module** (which has a _concrete_ imple&shy;mentation), an **EMS Interface** must remain _abstract_.

        {[in]<b>Interfaces</b> can't expose %[cs]$config% or %[cs]$proxy% objects &ndash; only abstract methods which suppliers implement.} 

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 I 18
      - mark 3 E 24
    focus: [3,1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

        {[ht,$peek]%[cu]BoardC% &ndash; A Quick Look}

        The {[cs]'@$distro/BoardC.em'} **Composite** imported by client programs will define the {[cu]OneShot} binding.

        {[cu]BoardC} imports {[bm,I]} a board-specific implementation of the abstract {[cu]OneShotI} **Interface**.

        Note how {[cu]BoardC} uses {[cu]OneShot} as an _alias_ which refer&shy;ences a **Module** source file of a different name.

        {[in]Click on the %[cu]OneShot% identifer and press <b>F12</b> to open the actual implementation file for &thinsp;<span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>&thinsp; <span class="em">Board</span>&thinsp;.}

        {[cu]Boards} will then explicitly {[ck]export} {[cu]OneShot}{[bm,E]},&thinsp; making its binding visible to clients of this **Composite**.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 C 38
      - mark 1 D 29
    focus: [1,1]
    acts: [$build]
    text: |

        {[ht,$build]%[cu]OneShot2P% &ndash; Build&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Load}

        Click the {[cb,build]} action button to execute the {[cu]OneShot2P} program on our target {[em]%[ci,circuit-board]%&nbsp;Board}&thinsp;.

        While similar to other programs we've seen exe&shy;cute, the {[cu]OneShot2P} signal capture paints a different picture.

        Triggered by an underlying timer interrupt, {[cf]handler} uses `DbgC` {[bm,C]} to mark this point of awakening.

        Dispatched by {[cu]FiberMgr} (after _re-enabling_ interrupts), the {[cf]blinkFB} fiber body marks its entry using `DbgD` {[bm,D]}.

        The time between {[bm,C]} and {[bm,D]} corresponds to our pro&shy;gram's _interrupt latency_ &ndash; which we'll measure next.


        

  - cmds:
      - mark 4 A 10
      - mark 4 B 13
      - mark 4 C 21
      - mark 4 D 27
    focus: [4,1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

        {[ht,$peek]%[cu]OneShot1P% &ndash; Details}




#         The {[cu]OneShot1P} program illustrates how to handle in&shy;coming timer events that awaken the MCU.
# 
#         In response to these events &ndash; programmed to occur *once* in the future &ndash; {[cu]OneShot1P} will blink an LED.
# 
#         Like the {[cu]Button1P} program toured earlier, {[cu]OneShot1P} uses a lower-level handler in lieu of a fiber object.

# 
#   - cmds:
#       - build 1 -l
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$build]⟪[cu]Ex08_OneShot1P⟫ &ndash; build + load}
# 
#         After building and loading completes, {[cu]OneShot1P} blinks {[cx]appLed} five times before halting.
# 
#         The blinks themselves occur every 100ms, as {[cu]OneShot1P} programmatically controls at run-time.
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - mark 1 A 7
#       - mark 1 B 15
#       - mark 1 C 17
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$details]⟪[cu]Ex08_OneShot1P⟫ &ndash; details}
# 
#         <div class="em-bmlist">
# 
#         {[bm,A]} The distro's {[cu]McuC} composite exports {[cu]OneShot}, which manages millisecond-resolution timer events.
# 
#         {[bm,A]} {[cu]OneShot} inherits the {[cn]em.hal}/{[cu]OneShotI} interface, which we'll view later in this tour.
# 
#         {[bm,B]} {[cu]OneShot1P} declares a private {[cf]handler} function, called when the current wakeup time has passed.
# 
#         {[bm,C]} {[cu]OneShot1P} also declares a private {[cn]done} flag, used to coordinate the main program loop with {[cf]handler}.
# 
#         </div>
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - mark 1 D 29
#       - mark 1 E 38
#     focus: [1,21]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$details]⟪[cu]Ex08_OneShot1P⟫ &ndash; more details}
# 
#         <div class="em-bmlist">
# 
#         {[bm,D]} {[cu]OneShot1P} enables the timer at run-time, specifying the number of millisecs before the wakeup event.
# 
#         {[bm,D]} The 2nd parameter of {[cf]enable} specifies a {[ct]Handler} function, which {[cu]OneShotMill} calls upon expiration.
# 
#         {[bm,E]} The locally-defined {[cf]handler} routine simply sets a private {[cn]done} flag &ndash; checked within {[cf]em$run}.
# 
#         </div>
# 
#         Note that {[cu]OneShot1P} declares {[cn]flag} as {[ck]volatile} &ndash; ef&shy;fectively synchronizing access to this private variable.
# 
#   - cmds:
#       - open 2
#     focus: [2,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$peek]⟪[cu]OneShotI⟫ &ndash; quick peek}
# 
#         This interface should require little explanation, based on what you've already seen in the {[cu]OneShot1P} program.
# 
#         Do note the optional {[cn]arg} paramater of {[cf]enable} &ndash; which defaults to {[ck]null} when not supplied.
# 
#         A later tour in this series will illustrate a practical use-case for this optional parameter.
# 
#   - cmds: []
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$extra]⟪[cu]Ex08_OneShot1P⟫ &ndash; extra credit}
# 
#         {[bi,task_alt]} Study the logic capture(s) found {[le,https://docs.openem.org/using/using-02/#tour-07-timer-handlers]here}.
# 
#         {[bi,task_alt]} Clone {[cu]OneShot1P} into your personal bundle.
# 
#         {[bi,task_alt]} Refactor {[cf]em$run} to delay _before_ blinking {[cx]appLed}.
# 
#         {[bi,sentiment_satisfied]} Happy coding&thinsp;!!!
# 
#   - cmds: []
#     focus: [1,1]
#     text: |
# 
#         {[ht,$steps]⟪[cu]Ex08_OneShot1P⟫ &ndash; next steps}
# 
#         Like the {[cu]Button1P}&thinsp;&rarr;&thinsp;{[cu]Button2P} transformation seen ear&shy;lier, **Tour&thinsp;08** re-implements {[cu]OneShot1P} using fibers.
#         
# 
# 